00:00:05.590 --> 00:00:07.510 align:start position:0%
eu justice ministers are set to discuss  new rules to better protect personal  information about european citizens at a  meeting in luxembourg on the 7th of  october  the ministers will look notably at the  so-called one-stop shop principle  it would see only one national authority  monitor businesses or organizations to  process personal data in more than one  country ideally that would be the  authority in the state where the  business operates  european efforts to combat  counterfeiting of the euro and other  money is also on the agenda  the ministers hope to agree a common  approach on the definition of criminal  offences and sanctions when it comes to  counterfeit money on october 8 home  affairs ministers will study an interim  report by the european commission on the  free movement of people after a group of  nations expressed concern about possible  abuses  the report looks at whether any rules  were broken and how the problem if there  is one might be avoided in the future  the ministers will also discuss the  protection of refugees who have fled the  conflict in syria according to un  figures more than two million syrians  have taken refuge in neighbouring  countries  in the margins of the talks ministers  will meet with their counterparts from  armenia azerbaijan belarus georgia  moldova and ukraine to discuss  cooperation in the justice and home  affairs sectors it comes ahead of an  eastern partnership summit in vilnius at  the end of november
 

